 I live in Los Angeles, where I work on a project

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.400
 at Stanford University called Big Local News

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.960
 and at the Los Angeles Times.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.480
 Thank you so much for joining us, Mary Jo and Ben.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.720
 We're really happy to have you both here today to talk really

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.400
 about local data journalism and how it's

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.080
 growing around the country, and I think around the world

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:23.600
 as well.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.960
 But I would love you to talk a little bit first about how

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.560
 you got into this world.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.400
 Now, Ben, when I was at The Guardian back in the day,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.980
 when we were setting up the data blog,

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.280
 we were definitely looking at the work

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.400
 that you guys were doing.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.160
 And I feel like you were actually really

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.040
 one of the innovators in this field.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.640
 So talk to me a little bit about how

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.920
 you got into data journalism and the kind of work that you do.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.600
 Well, it definitely wasn't the plan.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.000
 It wasn't anything that happened on purpose,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.960
 but I'm really glad it did.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.280
 I was a pretty listless undergraduate in Chicago,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.480
 Illinois at DePaul University and kind of unclear

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.360
 of where I was going with my life and career,

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.040
 like perhaps many undergraduates that Alberto

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.440
 encounters from time to time, and maybe himself as well.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.160
 And I won't bore you with the whole long story,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.840
 but through sort of an unusual series of events,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.340
 I became the assistant to two local television journalists

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.240
 in Chicago, who were both excellent practitioners.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.280
 And I became what was known at that time

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.040
 as the foot man, which was a very glorified term

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.480
 for the intern or the helper around the office.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.240
 And so as they prepare a weekly newspaper column

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.920
 and television piece, I would do research and scheduling

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.920
 and a little bit of television editing too.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.400
 And in that, I kind of caught the journalism bug.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.320
 And one of the early stories we worked on

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.320
 was about corruption in a Chicago suburb.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.640
 Locals could probably guess which ones.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.040
 And we did my first public records request

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.720
 for legal fees from a sort of local heavy

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.000
 who was milking the city out of money.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.880
 And it came as a really, really long printout.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.280
 Remember when printouts used to have the perforated tears

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:02.640
 along each side and a single print over 20, 30 pages

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.040
 will be connected through these long strips in the side

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.000
 that you have to rip off, right?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.160
 And we got that back, this epic printout

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.160
 of all the legal fees.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.500
 And we thought, well, what are we gonna do with this?

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.480
 How are we gonna turn this into a story?

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.260
 And I don't even know from where,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.800
 but I got the idea of just typing it into a spreadsheet.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.220
 I think it was probably the first spreadsheet

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.600
 of my entire life.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.760
 And I spent having nothing better to do.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.640
 I spent a good chunk of time just hammering all that in

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.560
 and we added it up.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.640
 And at the end, that number got read out on the local news.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.600
 The anchor even climbed up a step ladder

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.640
 and dropped the sort of accordion of paper live on camera,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.800
 which was kind of a funny little gag.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.160
 And I kind of caught the bug.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.000
 You know what I mean?

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.360
 And within that, I saw that through technology and data,

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.840
 it was kind of like a way I could help.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.880
 It's something I could chip in and do

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.280
 as a place for me to contribute.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.480
 And it also was a gateway to doing more ambitious journalism

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.400
 than just the average run of the mill story.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.200
 We did a story nobody else was doing

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.560
 that was more ambitious and analytical,

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.980
 and that was exciting.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.960
 And so I kind of just got into it there.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.280
 This is now 20 years ago, I guess,

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.040
 or more than that actually.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.400
 And I just followed that and followed that.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.240
 And without boring you with my whole life story,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:17.340
 I mean, I kind of ended up on this podcast today.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.880
 - Mary Jo, tell us about how you caught the bug.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.520
 - Mine is very much a local news-based situation.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.080
 I was, I don't know, three or four years into my career

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.840
 working as a reporter in Oshkosh, Wisconsin,

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:33.660
 a very small daily paper.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.800
 I was tasked with covering the police and sheriff's department

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.640
 both on a kind of a policy standpoint,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.480
 but also, you know, I went out to the crime scenes

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.600
 and the fires and whatnot.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.860
 But I was covering the city budget

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.600
 and it looked like the police department

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.480
 was growing its budget dramatically one year to the next.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.080
 And I was sitting there with a calculator

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.880
 trying to calculate the percent change

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.160
 between the two years for all of the departments.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.680
 And a colleague of mine came over and said,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.320
 do you know what Excel is?

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.680
 And I was like, no, no idea.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:06.600
 He opened it up.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.640
 He showed me how to do a simple percent change formula.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.020
 My eyes were bugging out.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.600
 I was like, wow, this is so cool.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.600
 Where did you learn this?

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.840
 You see, he told me I went to this IRE&NICAR bootcamp

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.180
 and they teach you all this stuff called,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.520
 back then it was called computer-assisted reporting.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.760
 And I was immediately hooked

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.400
 and I decided I had to keep going.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.720
 And sometime later that year,

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:35.440
 I was able to attend a training that Sarah Cohen did.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.600
 She was the IRE trainer at the time.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.360
 And I remember sitting in this room.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:42.160
 I don't think it was a hands-on training.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:43.720
 My memory serves.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.400
 But I just remember being enthralled

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.320
 by everything she was saying

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.060
 and mind being blown by this entirely different way

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.280
 of reporting that I had not learned in college

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.440
 that my editors had not taught me in my early years.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.960
 And it was a complete turning point for me.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:02.800
 - That's amazing.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.920
 Both historical, welcome to the podcast in the first place.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.520
 So thank you for being here.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.680
 But the stories that you are telling

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.080
 are similar to some of the stories

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:12.840
 that I tell my students that

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.960
 when you see data journalism or my own field,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.360
 data visualization from the outside,

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.400
 it looks like these very shiny, super advanced,

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.040
 high technology kind of endeavor.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.400
 But when you see how this software is made,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.600
 it's actually sometimes pretty pedestrian, right?

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.320
 It's like you essentially type the data

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.880
 into an Excel spreadsheet

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.520
 or you don't use machine learning algorithm.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:40.160
 No, you just use a simple Excel pivot table, right?

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.320
 So I find all that absolutely fascinating.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.360
 I mean, we are going to go back and forth

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.980
 between your professional careers,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.980
 but also other sites of your professional endeavors.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.140
 I'm really curious about your educational endeavors

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.720
 because both of you have been involved

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.760
 in educational initiatives, right?

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:02.140
 Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:02.980
 Both of you?

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:10.040
 - You mean what training we have taken or the teaching?

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.360
 - The training that you offer,

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.240
 because for example, Mary Jo,

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.960
 you have a data journalism academy, right?

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.480
 So both of you have been involved

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:18.720
 in these types of initiatives.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.840
 Not only, I mean, you didn't take advantage

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:23.320
 as of things such as like IRE NYCAR,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.760
 but you also offered to the community.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.280
 - Yes, I am a huge believer in teaching.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.520
 I think it has helped me

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.080
 as much as it helps the other people

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:38.760
 because I believe very much in the learn, do, teach approach.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.440
 I found all the holes in my knowledge

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:44.480
 by having to turn around and teach it to somebody else.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.200
 So I've also gotten so much back from other people.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.440
 That's how I learned.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.400
 So I feel the need to turn around

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.000
 and teach it to other people.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.480
 I have taught for about 10 years as an adjunct

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.240
 at the University of Minnesota,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.720
 a data journalism class that was fully dedicated

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.200
 just to data journalism.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.080
 I'm currently a senior fellow

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.040
 with the Center for Health Journalism's

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.800
 Data Journalism Program,

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:13.680
 in that we do a week-long training

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.120
 and then we do six months of mentoring

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.280
 and additional training throughout that time.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.600
 I'm particularly enjoying the mentoring part of that

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.980
 because it's another level of teaching.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.720
 You're helping them get a project off the ground.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.320
 You didn't just teach them how to run things in Excel

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:31.520
 and then walk away.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.000
 You keep it going and help them get a story out the door.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.720
 And it's quite fulfilling, actually.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:41.600
 And then I taught at IRE and Nicar a lot

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.720
 and I built up so many materials

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:47.800
 that I've gradually decided I should share those materials.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.120
 And at the start of the pandemic

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.080
 when I had a bunch of free time, I thought,

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.280
 you know what, it's time to turn some of these materials

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.640
 into videos.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:59.040
 So I made a new website and posted a lot of stuff there,

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.960
 mainly Excel and other teaching things.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.120
 I am doing a new thing right now

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.040
 that I'm actually gonna present about this

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.880
 at the Nicar conference this year.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:13.820
 I've created a little data for editors training module

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:16.040
 for the assignment editors in our room.

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:18.080
 And so these are the people who work directly

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.600
 with the reporters, mainly in our local news section.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.360
 And I've broken it down.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.080
 I'm not teaching them how to use Excel

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.180
 or how to run data or any of that.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:31.600
 I've broken it down into little sections

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.880
 of the different points in a data-driven story

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:39.520
 that the editor needs to weigh in and look at

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.240
 and what they should be doing at each point

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:43.480
 and what they should be looking for.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:48.280
 So like the key ones are that brainstorming process

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:49.760
 with their reporters when they're talking

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.000
 about what story should I do?

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.640
 What should I tackle?

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:56.060
 Looking, having, I'm giving them some clues

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.360
 that they should be watching for

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.560
 that maybe there's a data opportunity here.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:04.120
 What questions they should be asking, things like that.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.200
 And then the bulletproofing process.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:07.480
 Okay, the data has come in.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:08.440
 We've got findings.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:10.680
 We're showing you a bunch of charts and tables.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.400
 What do you do with it?

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.640
 Too often the editors just look at it and go,

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:15.800
 oh, that's pretty.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.280
 Oh, that's kind of interesting.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.140
 And I'm trying to teach them like,

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:20.920
 how about we take it to another level

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.320
 and you really help bulletproof it.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:28.080
 And then the third level is the editing the written story.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.440
 One of the things I've seen a lot as editors

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:33.200
 see a paragraph full of numbers

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.720
 and they just glaze right over it.

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.360
 And they don't think about like,

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:38.720
 what are we really saying?

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.640
 What are the key points we're getting across?

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.680
 So I'm trying to teach them some editing things around,

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.600
 you know, what's the key finding?

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:50.480
 You know, not lumping too many numbers in a paragraph.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.080
 All these kinds of things to try to help

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.880
 make the story more powerful.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:57.060
 And then we're also gonna do a graphic session.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.920
 My colleague at the head of our graphics department

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:00.160
 is gonna help me with that.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.120
 And that'll be more about, okay, when should we use graphics?

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.080
 What kinds of graphics should we use?

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.200
 What should you as an editor be doing

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.360
 when we send you those proofs?

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.200
 Instead of just saying, oh, that looks beautiful.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.340
 What else should you be doing?

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:18.340
 And I'm really anxious to see what kind of outcome that gets.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:24.080
 If it leads to more data-driven stories on a beat basis

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.400
 and more daily basis or a weekly basis,

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:31.000
 not just the big enterprise kind of stories.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:31.840
 - What about you, Ben?

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.680
 Can I talk a little bit about your educational efforts?

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.080
 - Well, I think Mary Jo said something

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:37.740
 very sort of succinct and wise there

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:40.840
 with the learn D2, learn, do, teach formulation.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:41.680
 You know what I mean?

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:42.520
 It's a great way.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:43.720
 It definitely benefits yourself.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:45.280
 But I also think there's another great thing

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.480
 about that kind of approach or model

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.920
 is there's really a very sort of like stimulating

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.640
 and actually rewarding creative process

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.920
 of converting something that you've learned how to do

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:56.400
 into like a lesson or something

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.000
 that you can teach someone else.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.400
 And it actually is like, it kind of gets the neurons firing

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.860
 the same way when you're like kind of innovating the code

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.240
 in the first place for the idea.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.240
 And so for me along those lines, you know,

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:13.120
 one like sort of approach to teaching that I've enjoyed

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.520
 or found fruitful is taking emerging new techniques

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:17.880
 that we're developing in the newsroom

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.200
 that I feel like are kind of like a big deal for me

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:21.760
 or a big deal for our team,

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:23.680
 but that I don't see like being taught

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:25.040
 and then trying to convert that

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:27.660
 into like a night-card bootcamp or class

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.200
 to try to spread it to other people.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.360
 And so, you know, examples I've done this with not alone.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:33.840
 I always try to seek out other folks

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.420
 to help me like really strengthen the idea.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:38.640
 But with others, we've created some of the earliest

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.200
 night-card bootcamps on how to use Jupyter Notebooks,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.440
 how to use the static site frameworks

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:47.760
 that so many newsrooms use to make standalone visual stories

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.120
 but like nobody ever talks about, right?

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.680
 And this year at night-card, we're gonna have,

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:54.200
 I think the first kind of three hour bootcamp

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.240
 on how to automate a scraper

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:58.240
 within the GitHub actions framework.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.520
 And so to me, like those three things, Jupyter Notebooks,

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:04.320
 static site page building, Git scraping

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.760
 are three really important like technologies

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:08.440
 that have taken off in our world

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:09.640
 in the last five or 10 years.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.560
 And it's just been fun to try to like think about

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.920
 and pin down, well, how exactly do we use these?

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:16.560
 What are the fundamental skills?

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.600
 How can I pass that on to someone else?

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:21.080
 - It's really interesting to me

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:25.760
 how you're both so involved in teaching.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.680
 And I wonder, does that reflect towards,

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.680
 I wonder if this is particularly applies to people

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.400
 working with data on a local level,

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.540
 is often people are kind of isolated.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:39.140
 Often it's like one or two people on their own,

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.280
 they've got no support, there's no network.

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.160
 But the colleagues from other organizations

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:46.920
 are kind of struggling in the same way.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:49.200
 So there's a kind of a need there, is that fair?

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.480
 - I love the psychoanalytical take here.

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.080
 I mean, I did grow up as a lonely, nerdy kid

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:55.560
 on a grand full road side,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:57.560
 who didn't have a lot of friends.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:00.520
 And so maybe this is me still trying to connect,

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:02.080
 you know what I mean, and get over.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.080
 I was not crazy.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:06.680
 - Well, I think you're right.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.200
 There's so many that, you know,

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:12.140
 these days they refer to themselves as lonely coders.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:14.800
 The majority of my 20 years in data journalism

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.720
 was spent in a newsroom where I didn't have support

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:19.080
 from anybody else.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:20.860
 And so I sought it out from other people

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:22.780
 and learned from other people.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:24.440
 And then, you know, I turned around and taught.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:28.880
 But I think another factor is the Nykar community

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:32.400
 has always been a sharing community.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.360
 If you even look at how Nykar was formed in the first place,

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.460
 it was really a group of investigative reporters

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:43.880
 who realized they needed to learn how to work with data

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:45.640
 and they didn't know.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.480
 And a few of them learned somehow, I'm not sure how,

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.920
 and then they decided to share it with the others

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:52.040
 to try to pass it along.

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:54.840
 And so it was really, that's how Nykar got started.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.960
 And that has been infused throughout all the years.

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:00.920
 I remember so many boot camps,

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:02.720
 and I think Ben is guilty of this,

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:05.840
 of people sitting at the bar at the end of the conference

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:09.600
 helping somebody with their data, you know, hovering over

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:12.280
 a laptop, helping someone with their data

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:13.880
 when they should be, you know, having fun

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:14.960
 and enjoying themselves.

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:18.680
 But that's how much I think our community likes to share

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:22.120
 and teach each other and it just, it's wonderful.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:23.360
 - Yeah, and just to add to that,

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.720
 I mean, I think if you follow the origins back

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.760
 like Mary Jo was doing, I mean, data journalism's roots

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.300
 go a lot further than I think is commonly recognized.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:32.140
 You know what I mean?

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:34.080
 It's really a practice that stretches back decades

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:37.360
 into the invention of the computer in the '60s and '70s.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:40.000
 And I think is that culture of those early days

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.160
 is probably in like an anthropological sense,

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:47.040
 like indistinguishable from the hacker culture of MIT

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:48.460
 that's so celebrated.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:49.300
 You know what I mean?

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.780
 It really was a very similar people

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:55.400
 with very similar sort of ethos of sharing

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.000
 that is the wellspring for a lot of what we do today.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.040
 And I think that, you know, as an inheritor of that,

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.640
 I'm continuing to be inspired by it

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:08.400
 and I feel some obligation to uphold it, you know?

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:09.440
 - I think that we all do.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:11.480
 We all have the sort of like feel the need

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:14.560
 to contribute back to the community that helped us

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:16.900
 at the beginning of our career, right?

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.680
 I have a question related to what you were both saying

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:22.600
 about, you know, growing up as a nerdy kid, right?

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:25.440
 I sort of sympathize with that because, I mean,

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.320
 people don't see where I'm sitting right now,

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.720
 but I'm sitting in a room with my kid

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:33.640
 and then surrounded by comic books, science fiction novels,

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.560
 you know, books about history and psychology

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:38.220
 and then some tabletop games.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.980
 So the nerdery comes naturally to me.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:44.080
 So has that improved or has that changed?

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.680
 It's like, are we still seeing or are you still seeing

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.000
 as sort of like the nerds in the newsroom

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.020
 or has that situation changed

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:53.680
 and data journalism, data visualization,

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:55.280
 computer assistant reporting,

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:59.800
 has it has become more widespread and more accepted

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:02.360
 or more popular within local newsrooms?

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:03.760
 What do you think?

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.840
 - I think it is more widely accepted

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:11.760
 as a tool that every reporter should have in their toolbox.

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:13.840
 I like to talk about data analysis

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.640
 as really another form of interviewing a source.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:18.560
 That's all we're doing really.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:20.600
 And it just requires kind of a little bit

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.480
 of a foreign language to do it.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:25.320
 I also see a data journalists increasingly

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.560
 as the innovators in the room.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:30.440
 In all the newsrooms I've been in

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:32.200
 for quite a few years now,

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.400
 the data journalists have been at the forefront

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:38.200
 of whatever new digital innovation we're gonna do.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:41.120
 And they're the ones pushing for it and seeking it out.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.320
 And in my newsroom right now,

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.800
 my team is the one often called upon

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.840
 for like, what do we do next?

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:49.680
 Where do we go?

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.160
 And I think that's wonderful.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:54.680
 And I think that takes us out of the nerdy idea

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:58.640
 into kind of more of the leaders.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.880
 - Yeah, and you see this in the product realm too,

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:02.920
 where a lot of people out of the data journalism world

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.960
 have become leaders in the next generation

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:07.320
 of digital business products

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:08.600
 their companies are putting together,

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:11.680
 which is a whole other level beyond

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:13.840
 just like what's happening in the newsroom.

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:15.560
 To be frank with you,

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:17.040
 I think in the last two or three years,

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:21.120
 we've seen a quantum leap with data journalism in newsrooms.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:23.640
 The takeover has happened.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:27.280
 You see it at the prestige level with those metrics.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:29.400
 You look at the Pulitzer Prizes last year,

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.360
 five of the Pulitzer Prizes

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:33.320
 went to data journalism projects,

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:36.920
 including the public service highest award,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.640
 which went to the New York Times COVID tracking effort, right?

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:42.040
 Which on its own was a gigantic public service

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:46.320
 and an incredible feat of like data gathering and analysis.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.680
 But it also was the most popular

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.800
 and I'll say it most lucrative thing

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:54.880
 the New York Times has ever published.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.200
 And those COVID tracking things

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:57.720
 I bet at the Minneapolis paper

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:59.560
 was the most popular thing ever published.

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:00.800
 I'll just throw that out there.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:02.000
 Mary Jo can correct me.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.040
 It's the most popular thing

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.400
 the Washington Post ever published.

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:05.480
 It's the most popular thing

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.400
 the Los Angeles Times published.

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:10.680
 And I don't mean to demean the news by saying that,

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:13.440
 but once the product you're creating

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.200
 is both the high prestige thing

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:21.200
 and more importantly, the economic and economic engine.

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:22.120
 You know what I mean?

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:23.440
 That's growing the digital audience

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.760
 of which there's a desperate need to grow.

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:28.320
 The entire way the organization looks at you changes

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:29.560
 just fundamentally.

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:31.560
 And that's happening.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.240
 - Yes, the tracker we'd put up was,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.960
 is hands down the historically most read page

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:39.520
 in the history of the Star Tribune.

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:41.800
 And the numbers on it are so astronomical

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:46.680
 that I don't envision it being that record being broken.

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:49.120
 So it would take something pretty massive,

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:50.040
 I think to break it.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:52.800
 But the other interesting thing we saw

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:56.240
 is that it was also the most popular page

00:22:56.240 --> 00:23:00.920
 among our digital readers who are not subscribers,

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:02.920
 but they come to us pretty regularly

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:05.920
 and we would like them to be subscribers.

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:07.280
 We call them in tenders.

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:09.800
 And it was the most popular page among them.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:13.680
 And now we have conversations going around

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:17.480
 what else like that, maybe not data,

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:20.160
 but more in the service journalism realm,

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:22.600
 but data is one of the things you can do

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.360
 for service journalism.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.000
 What can we do to help get more of those people

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:28.960
 to become subscribers?

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:30.000
 - Exactly.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.840
 This all changed.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:32.400
 And Steve Kornacki was named

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.080
 one of the sexiest men alive guys.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.960
 Come on, we're on top.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:40.080
 Yeah, the dog agrees with that assessment.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:43.640
 So we have talked a lot about COVID on this podcast.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:46.640
 And I wonder what you think the longer term effects

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:48.880
 are gonna be for your field

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:52.920
 of that very data intensive experience

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:56.080
 of working with COVID data in a way that readers

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.880
 and users actually really responded to.

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:00.960
 - I mean, the tracker thing is here to stay.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:05.240
 And I think the creating dashboards for the general public

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:07.400
 on major topics they care about is not new.

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:09.680
 I mean, it's something that's been around with weather

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.680
 and sports for a long time.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:13.960
 But I think that it's a type of product

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:16.360
 that clearly our audience wants

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.360
 that is in my opinion, like capital J journalism

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:23.800
 in most circumstances, but requires a new assembly line

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:27.200
 to be constructed inside the metaphorical factory

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:29.280
 of your newsroom to create that.

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:30.120
 You know what I mean?

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:31.360
 It requires a little bit more

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.040
 of a product development mindset,

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:36.360
 a longer development cycle, a more iterative approach,

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:37.200
 you know what I mean?

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:40.360
 Than juggling what's on Sunday's front page

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:42.800
 and the sort of like day-to-day heartbeat of the newsroom.

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:44.520
 And so I think there's a way in which

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:49.080
 to produce more of those kinds of widgets,

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.600
 data journals and teams are gonna have to find a way

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.360
 to create a kind of app workflow

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:55.560
 that's able to support that.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.040
 And I think that's becoming more

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:00.000
 like a product development team in some ways.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.240
 - From a different perspective,

00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:05.360
 I think it's gonna have a long-term effect in newsrooms

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:07.600
 because I think it really woke up a lot of people

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:11.280
 to the need and benefits to being able

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:12.720
 to analyze your own data

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.000
 and not rely on a government official

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.680
 to hand you some numbers on a press release.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:18.880
 Especially in those early days

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:23.280
 where we kind of had to pull stuff together ourselves.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:27.840
 But then even more long-term is data literacy,

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.920
 increasing understanding among reporters and editors

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:33.080
 for the need to understand

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:35.640
 all the weird little caveats behind data

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:40.640
 and the problems we have with denominators and whatnot.

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:44.760
 And I think there's gonna be,

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:48.680
 I'm expecting to see greater appetite

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:51.480
 for data training in my newsroom.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:55.760
 - We talked a lot about how data journalism is affecting

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:57.200
 the bottom line for news organizations

00:25:57.200 --> 00:25:59.440
 and how they're all seeing the value now.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:02.440
 If you're a reader or a user,

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:03.440
 you don't necessarily realize

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:05.440
 you're looking at data journalism, I guess.

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.880
 But what difference does it make to them?

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:09.080
 Do you think it helps people understand

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:11.040
 the local areas better

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.720
 in ways that we haven't quite kind of grasped

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:17.160
 the long-term impact of yet?

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.120
 - Well, I think the way we present data journalism,

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:23.000
 especially in charts and graphs and things like that,

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:27.400
 allow people who maybe need a visual aid like that

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.640
 to see something rather than reading

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:31.440
 long sentences of text,

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.600
 that some readers just do better

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:37.400
 with absorbing information that way than others.

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.280
 And I think also the readers maybe

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:42.720
 who aren't naturally inclined to do that

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.520
 are getting better at it because there is so much of it.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:49.680
 - We all have, oh, sorry, Ben.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:50.800
 Were you going to say something?

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:52.440
 We can make a pause.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:54.280
 Let's set this up, okay, perfect.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:59.040
 I was about to say, let's begin there,

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.920
 we all have projects in our past as professionals

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:03.720
 that we are very fond of,

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.880
 even if they are not perhaps the best projects

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:06.840
 that we have ever done,

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:09.280
 but there are certain projects that we hold dear, right?

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.520
 So if you had to choose just one of your local

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:16.360
 data journalism projects in the past, right,

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.760
 what would that be and why?

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:19.600
 What was the story about?

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:21.920
 What do you do to gather the data or visualize

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:24.640
 or display the data, explain the data,

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:26.800
 and what type of impact that story had?

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:33.680
 - In 2018, we did a series called Denied Justice

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:36.760
 that looked at how the criminal justice system

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:40.640
 in Minnesota was handling sex assault investigations.

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:43.960
 A reporter came to me and said he was hearing

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:48.960
 that very few cases that get reported to police

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:53.000
 actually result in a conviction, but nobody tracks that.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.560
 It's not a number that someone calculates.

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:59.800
 So we put in requests for thousands of police

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:03.900
 investigative reports and we built our own database,

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:07.480
 tracking key bits of information off these cases

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:11.200
 and then hunting down, was this referred to the prosecutor?

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:12.640
 Were charges filed?

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:14.680
 Was somebody convicted?

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:18.920
 And a mountain of work in order to get a few numbers.

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:23.040
 And what we found is like 8% of reported cases

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:24.780
 result in a conviction.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:26.600
 And we ended up doing a series,

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.240
 it ended up being a nine part series,

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:29.880
 which we did not expect,

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:32.880
 because it ended up being so powerful.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:36.600
 The response was overwhelming from readers and policymakers.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:41.440
 And we had hundreds of victims come forward

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:45.280
 to share their stories because they read our first story

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.760
 and were like, oh my gosh, I'm not the only one

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:50.620
 who had this bad experience with the police department.

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:51.520
 I'm not the only one.

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:55.840
 This is so cathartic to hear this

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:57.580
 and they wanted to share their stories.

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:01.980
 And as a result, Minnesota's sexual assault laws

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:05.200
 have been overhauled quite substantially.

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:09.600
 Several police departments have added additional staff

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:14.280
 or like victim advocates embedded into their department,

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:16.240
 prosecutors embedded into the department

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.400
 to help improve the quality of the investigations.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:24.040
 And it was one of the most,

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:26.280
 probably hands down the most rewarding

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.240
 piece of work I've ever done.

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:32.340
 At the end of it, we gathered all these victims

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:34.840
 at the state Capitol and took a picture

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:36.960
 of all of them together.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:40.560
 And these women came out in the bitter cold in Minnesota

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.800
 in December to do this photo.

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:45.380
 And they came up to me and the other reporters

00:29:45.380 --> 00:29:48.080
 and they were so thankful.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.720
 And I've never had that experience before

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:53.700
 that the people just thanking us for the work.

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:57.920
 And that story is a really big combination

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:01.880
 of data being the spine holding up the key findings

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:05.920
 and then these powerful emotional stories from victims.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:09.420
 And policy makers told us that the two things combined

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:13.700
 were so powerful that they could not ignore it.

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:16.960
 - Yeah, when the data comes together

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:19.000
 with real life in a way, you know what I mean?

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.840
 Or when the data leads you to the doorstep

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:23.980
 you didn't know you needed to go to, right?

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:27.760
 That is just this sort of like unforgettable feeling.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:28.600
 You know what I mean?

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:31.600
 It's kind of like one of the deeper work hits.

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:33.560
 It's better than any hack, you know what I mean?

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:36.300
 And so like for me, like an example of that was

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.520
 we had a circumstance where there was like

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:40.920
 a little political fight in LA government

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:44.000
 over the fire department that was really pretty superficial

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:45.760
 and was just a couple of people

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:47.480
 trying to score political points.

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:49.900
 But it raised some questions about the efficacy

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:52.720
 of the 911 system in the city of Los Angeles.

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.040
 And so my colleague and I, Robert Lopez,

00:30:55.040 --> 00:30:59.320
 decided to get a copy of the entire 911 calls database

00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:02.040
 and analyze what was happening with the 911 system.

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:05.000
 And you know, there's a lot of ins and outs

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:05.840
 to how it all worked out.

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:07.400
 But the reality was is by doing our own

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:09.480
 independent analysis of the data,

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:12.640
 we were able to identify several structural flaws

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.640
 in how the 911 center operates,

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:17.000
 how they handle calls near the city border

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:18.640
 with neighboring fire departments,

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:21.720
 and then go out into the real world tracking real calls,

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:23.800
 knock on doors and find out what happened.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:26.760
 And in that we discovered these kind of deeper problems

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:28.760
 with the system that nobody was talking about

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.000
 in city politics.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.480
 And we were able to really point to where the system

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:35.960
 needed to improve to like make a difference.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:39.920
 And then a change actually did happen, you know.

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:44.000
 And to me, that's sort of like that feeling of

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:45.960
 where the statistics lead you to the store,

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:47.520
 you listen to the numbers,

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:49.760
 and then you follow them out into the real world.

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:51.960
 And then you connect that with what's really happening there.

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:54.160
 And then you bring that back to your audience.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:55.560
 That's better than any chart.

00:31:55.560 --> 00:31:56.400
 That's better.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:57.440
 You know, I love charts, you know what I mean?

00:31:57.440 --> 00:32:00.720
 But like, that's better than any little dinky thing.

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:02.720
 That's the deeper like thing

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:05.560
 that numbers and statistics can lead you to,

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.000
 which is the sort of hidden truth,

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.920
 but the deeper story that isn't being told.

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:14.440
 And that's the hit, you know,

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:17.240
 I'm still looking for it on every story to get back to.

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:18.440
 I'm still chasing.

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:20.960
 - Those are both examples of stories where a human

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:23.480
 is probably not gonna come forward and tell us

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.200
 that there's a story there.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:26.400
 Maybe they'd have an inkling,

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:29.720
 but probably nobody did the data analysis

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.160
 to know for sure that that's what it is.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:36.520
 And it takes us to do these kinds of lifts

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:40.280
 to heavy lifts on data to find those stories.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:43.040
 And, you know, as I said, interview the source

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:47.240
 to get at the meat of that story that needs to be told.

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:49.360
 - And then sometimes I think this may have happened

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:50.440
 in Mary Jo's case too.

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.440
 Your finding, your synthesis of what's really going on

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:56.520
 actually gives a name to and some meaning to something

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:58.440
 really negative that happened to somebody.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:00.440
 And then they decide, then your sources

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:01.440
 and the people you're writing about

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.120
 find this deeper reward in the whole thing.

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:05.960
 And it's very rare, you know what I mean?

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:07.880
 But to me, that's as good as it gets.

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:13.720
 - That combination of like traditional reporting

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:17.960
 with the data and that sweet spot event have impact as well

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:20.720
 is just everything you can ask for, isn't it?

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:24.360
 So we are in February, this is our first part of the year.

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:27.080
 If you two were to make predictions,

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:28.240
 I know everybody does make predictions,

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:29.880
 but you two make predictions

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:33.720
 about what your big exercise is gonna be this year.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:36.440
 - Let's let Mary Jo off the hook here.

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:38.400
 I don't want you to make her pop up too much.

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:40.680
 So since I'm working on this new project at Stanford

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:41.680
 called Big Local News,

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.520
 which is really an open source collaborative effort

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:48.600
 to try to be a data backbone for all the local newsrooms

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:53.120
 who maybe need help or some consulting

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:57.000
 or some technology to really pull off big data projects,

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.520
 we're looking to take it up a notch, Simon.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.240
 I mean, I think this year, this team I'm on

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:04.800
 with Cheryl and Surdar and everybody up on the farm

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.960
 in Palo Alto, I'll say it here,

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:09.400
 we are going to build the biggest web scrapers

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:11.240
 in the history of data journalism.

00:34:11.240 --> 00:34:13.840
 We're gonna, we have a project called Agenda Watch

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:16.920
 where we're gonna scrape hundreds of city council agendas

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:19.280
 across the country, put them into one database,

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:20.840
 and we're gonna try to do the same thing

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:22.400
 with county courthouses.

00:34:22.400 --> 00:34:24.480
 And you know, those types of projects,

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:27.440
 scraping your local county courthouse or city council,

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:29.360
 I mean, every local newspaper in America

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:31.160
 has wanted to do it.

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:34.040
 Some of them have tried to do it.

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:36.520
 A very few have succeeded in doing it

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:38.040
 for like a short period of time,

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:40.640
 but I just don't think that there's been a lasting

00:34:40.640 --> 00:34:44.360
 and sort of broad solution to that challenge

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:48.400
 because it's a lot of work, there's competing priorities,

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:50.840
 you know, da, da, da, da, da.

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:53.680
 And we're really looking to solve that or try to

00:34:53.680 --> 00:34:56.160
 by being the sort of database library

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:58.080
 that attacks those big things.

00:34:58.080 --> 00:35:00.000
 And so by December 31st,

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.600
 we'll be the biggest scraper you've ever seen,

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:05.040
 outside of Google, of course.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:06.640
 - I love the Local News Project,

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:08.160
 I'm really glad to hear that.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.740
 (upbeat music)

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:16.880
 Okay, so we are gonna finish off with a new section,

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:19.440
 which I'm gonna just call no dumb questions

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:21.720
 because these are quite dumb questions.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.200
 And they're one word answers,

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:25.080
 but I'm gonna go first.

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:28.640
 And my one to Mary Jo is,

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:32.240
 what book or class had an impact on you

00:35:32.240 --> 00:35:33.480
 as a professional?

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:37.040
 - The Investigative Reporter's Handbook.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:40.000
 - Very good, how about you, Ben?

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:42.240
 - Oh, just one?

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.320
 - Yeah, just one, I'm afraid, sorry.

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.120
 - Oh, man.

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:47.880
 - Be concise, we are all journalists,

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:48.720
 we are supposed to be concise.

00:35:48.720 --> 00:35:51.000
 - Gotta be concise, all right, I'm gonna do it.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.280
 I will say the one that had an effect on me,

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:56.760
 I will say The Autobiography of Malcolm X

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:58.040
 by Alex Haley, guys.

00:35:58.040 --> 00:35:58.880
 How about that?

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:00.640
 It's like a young teenage me,

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:03.360
 you know what I mean, moving from the country to Chicago,

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:05.200
 getting a broader view of the world.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:06.760
 It was nonfiction journalism

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:09.120
 that helped me like see what was up,

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:11.120
 you know, and its book is an alternative version

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:14.080
 of American history that I hadn't heard before.

00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:16.480
 It's also a religious conversion story,

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:18.600
 not unlike one I've been taught in church,

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:21.000
 you know, as a kid who went to Sunday school.

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.400
 And it shows the power of stories

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.040
 that go against the grain

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:27.760
 that aren't otherwise being told.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:29.480
 And it's a great piece of literature.

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:31.720
 So if you haven't read it, you know,

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:33.040
 you should check it out.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:36.960
 I think sadly in some ways, you know,

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:38.960
 we've regressed since then.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:40.760
 You know, that was a time when Alex Haley

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:43.360
 could have the most popular show on television

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:46.200
 and write a really provocative book.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:48.320
 And as a reminder of the power,

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:52.800
 and I would say virtue of the mainstream media,

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:55.320
 when it's properly focused, you know.

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:57.560
 - Those were the times.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:01.160
 Okay, next quick question for both of you, actually.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:04.760
 So if you could keep just one of the tools

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.240
 that you commonly use in your daily job,

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:09.600
 what would that tool be?

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:10.440
 - R.

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:13.600
 - I like that answer.

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:14.600
 What about you, Ben?

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:17.000
 - Oh, a spreadsheet, guys.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:18.920
 It all begins with a spreadsheet.

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:20.880
 You can't play baseball without a bat

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:23.200
 and you can't crush data without a spreadsheet.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:24.040
 And--

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:27.560
 - Oh, you can, you can, R can do everything.

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:30.960
 There's so many things that R can do that Excel can't.

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:33.240
 - These R people, they really love R.

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:34.280
 Have you guys noticed this?

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:35.520
 Like, I think--

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:37.920
 - I have actually noticed that.

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:40.200
 - Yeah, I think that, you know,

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.920
 not unlike, that it's like R conversion stories.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:45.040
 It has an evangelical fervor and flavor,

00:37:45.040 --> 00:37:46.600
 not unlike the Nation of Islam.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:50.160
 - You are only saying that because you're a Python person.

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:52.280
 That's the only reason why you're saying that.

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:55.240
 I thought you were gonna say Python for sure.

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:57.280
 - No, I think it all comes down to the fundamentals.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:37:58.360
 You know, you can't dribble.

00:37:58.360 --> 00:37:59.920
 You can't play basketball if you can't dribble.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:02.560
 Can't play baseball without a bat.

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:03.920
 I'll give you the religious flavor,

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:06.120
 is I'll pick Libre Office's Calc,

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:08.320
 the open source free alternative spreadsheet,

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:12.120
 which is just as good as the ones from those companies

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:13.560
 you maybe have heard of.

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:16.120
 - Okay, if you rephrase the question to

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:19.080
 what should any journalist have a tool,

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:20.720
 I would say Excel.

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:23.920
 But what should I, as a professional data journalist,

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:27.760
 doing this full-time, crunching numbers like non-stopped,

00:38:27.760 --> 00:38:28.600
 then it's R.

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:33.040
 - All right, okay.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:35.600
 Next one, what is your favorite hack

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:38.200
 that you are too embarrassed to admit?

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.840
 I can tell you my one is,

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:42.040
 and I still do this occasionally,

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:45.680
 is the way you can use the finder and place

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:48.640
 in Microsoft Word as a shortcut to getting stuff done

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:49.840
 if you're in a hurry.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:50.680
 I still use it.

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:51.680
 I'm slightly embarrassed about it.

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:53.760
 So there you go, that's mine.

00:38:53.760 --> 00:38:54.920
 Ben, do you go first?

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:56.360
 - Well, I'm not easily embarrassed,

00:38:56.360 --> 00:39:01.360
 which maybe is a bug, not a feature in my personality.

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:05.400
 But I mean, a hack,

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:08.600
 saying no to other people in the newsroom.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:09.440
 You know what I mean?

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:10.840
 I think that the data journalists,

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:12.200
 especially in smaller newsrooms,

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:17.200
 can sometimes get consumed by helping things

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:19.200
 just go the way they've always been going

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:21.040
 or sort of chipping in around the edges.

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:23.520
 And I think that being a helper

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:26.600
 and assisting that is noble work and often really valuable.

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:30.560
 But I see it pulled back a lot of other data journalists

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:33.280
 from really reaching their potential from taking risks

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:35.880
 and from doing the more ambitious, powerful work

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:37.640
 that our skills are capable of.

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:39.720
 So never be afraid to say no

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:44.680
 and never be afraid to bet on yourself

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:46.000
 and to take a chance.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:47.880
 - I guess I don't know if I'd call this a hack,

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.320
 but I am probably most embarrassed to admit

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:53.840
 that I have data import scripts

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:57.840
 that I have not automated, but I probably should.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:39:59.640
 And I just still keep running them.

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:03.160
 It's not exactly by hand, but they're not automated.

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:06.120
 I feel like I should be moving into the 21st century,

00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:07.760
 but, oh well.

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.560
 - Well, I still run out of patience

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:13.840
 whenever I need to style graphs or charts in R

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.840
 and I export them as PDFs and I style them in Illustrator

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:18.680
 just because I find it faster.

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:20.400
 So that would be my personal hack.

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:22.360
 And I'm not embarrassed at all in admitting it.

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:25.920
 So we all have a skeletons in our closet, I guess.

00:40:27.080 --> 00:40:29.520
 - I had trouble making a graphic in R yesterday

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:33.080
 and I was like, ugh, okay, I'm just gonna put it in Excel

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:34.520
 and I will make it in Excel.

00:40:34.520 --> 00:40:36.280
 - I do the same thing with Illustrator.

00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:38.800
 It's like R is not working, fine.

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:40.760
 Let me go to the Illustrator's graphing tool,

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:44.160
 which is sort of like the clunkiest graphing tool ever.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:49.160
 So anyway, so let's go to the next one.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:50.960
 What would you have done

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:53.480
 if you hadn't gone into journalism?

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:55.480
 - Oh, MG, I have no idea.

00:40:55.480 --> 00:40:59.640
 I've wanted to be a journalist since I was 16, no clue.

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:03.040
 - Well, I was not in a very motivated place

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:05.720
 before I found journalism as we covered already, Alberto.

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:09.320
 But one thing I did like to do at that time in my young life

00:41:09.320 --> 00:41:13.600
 was read pretentious books and be like a pretentious student.

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:16.360
 And so I think at that time, if you had asked me,

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:17.680
 I probably would have told you

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:20.120
 I would have become a high school English teacher

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:21.400
 or something, you know what I mean?

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:25.680
 And I don't think that that's a crazy outcome for me.

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:28.640
 And maybe I would have been happy doing that.

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:29.480
 It's hard to say.

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:31.200
 I probably would have stayed in Chicago

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:34.600
 and now that I think about it, never met my wife,

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:37.560
 which is making me sad to even contemplate.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:40.280
 Like, you know, that's probably it.

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:42.280
 - So glad you didn't do that, Ben.

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:43.720
 (laughing)

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:44.760
 - Oh my God.

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:46.720
 My monologue to like high school students

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:49.840
 about of mice and men, it would be just terrible.

00:41:49.840 --> 00:41:51.920
 - I would hope I need to hear that.

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:55.240
 Okay, last one, pie charts or tree maps.

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:57.680
 And I'm going to go to Mary Jo for this one.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:41:58.520
 - Tree maps.

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:02.120
 - Like anything with visualizations, it depends.

00:42:02.120 --> 00:42:02.960
 You know what I mean?

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:04.880
 Like, so like, I would say like,

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:07.440
 well, at first I would say never pie charts

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:08.840
 in the circular form.

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:11.720
 However, if you have like a single,

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:14.240
 like a categorical thing,

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:17.720
 I think the like underrated pie chart alternative

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:19.800
 is a single stacked bar.

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:20.640
 You know what I mean?

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:23.000
 Just like one rectangle sliced up.

00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:25.680
 You can do the nice little labels on the top and bottom.

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:27.640
 And I guess it's technically a stacked bar chart,

00:42:27.640 --> 00:42:28.560
 but it's just a single bar.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.400
 It's also a pie chart.

00:42:30.400 --> 00:42:31.440
 I think those are great.

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:33.680
 I think those really work and you can stack them.

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:35.440
 It doesn't like Pew do that on their quizzes.

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:36.280
 I think that's good.

00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:38.800
 Cause like, cause maybe that is a tree map.

00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:39.640
 I don't know.

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:41.680
 You guys tell me in some way it is a tree map, I guess,

00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:43.720
 but like to me the tree map works

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.800
 when there's a lot of slices,

00:42:45.800 --> 00:42:48.760
 like there's like 50 slices

00:42:48.760 --> 00:42:51.720
 and like one or two of them are huge, you know?

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:53.240
 And you want to be like, these ones are big

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:54.600
 and these ones are small.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:57.640
 But like, if you only have like five things, you know,

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:00.480
 you don't need that vertical part of the tree map.

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:05.080
 If it's just like a fixed wide height, it works fine.

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:06.960
 Sorry, I just did that.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:08.560
 But that's really how I feel about it.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:10.080
 - It's a great, it's a great answer.

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:14.960
 I mean, tree maps were created to show a nested hierarchies,

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:15.800
 right?

00:43:15.800 --> 00:43:16.640
 So you have a total

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:19.400
 and then you subdivide that total into large divisions,

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:21.280
 for example, population of the world.

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:23.440
 And then you start dividing them into continents

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:26.000
 and each one of the continent pieces

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:28.240
 gets further subdivided into the country.

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:29.960
 So that's what tree maps are for.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:31.040
 So it's an excellent answer.

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:33.800
 I would advocate for the pie chart though.

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:36.120
 So just to be the contrarian,

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.720
 I would vote for pie charts if I could.

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:38.960
 Simon, can I do that?

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:42.640
 - You don't get advocate for pie charts.

00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:45.000
 - I did not expect that, Alberto.

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:48.400
 - I'm getting old.

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:51.080
 Getting older, you know, on a simple pie chart.

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:54.560
 I tweeted the other day, if a graphic works, it's good.

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:59.040
 And pie charts unfortunately are fortunate, often work.

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:01.360
 It's like if there are two, three subdivisions,

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:04.240
 four subdivisions perhaps, readers like them,

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:07.200
 readers understand them, readers read them,

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:08.720
 they bring attention to the data.

00:44:08.720 --> 00:44:11.720
 So, you know, I'm getting older, more flexible,

00:44:11.720 --> 00:44:13.320
 more pragmatic in some sense.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.200
 So go for the pie chart.

00:44:15.200 --> 00:44:17.200
 - Even the three-dimensional pie chart?

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:18.280
 - No, three-dimensional.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:19.800
 That's going to, maybe when I'm 80.

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:21.240
 - That's the limit so far.

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:23.760
 - Yeah, that's, no, maybe when I'm about to retire,

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:25.880
 maybe I will accept three pie charts.

00:44:25.880 --> 00:44:29.360
 - Wow, now that Alberto has been switched with somebody else,

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:30.640
 I think that's a good point to end.

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:34.160
 Ben Welsh, MaryJo Webster, thank you so much.

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:35.240
 - Thank you.

00:44:35.240 --> 00:44:36.640
 - Thank you for having us.

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:41.320
 (upbeat music)

00:45:08.720 --> 00:45:11.320
 (upbeat music)

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:41.300
 (upbeat music)
